From 5bd815db4eb2e2be09f6ec588d7e00e4550fc60d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael John <mj@tierone.software>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 11:55:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] sed: skip the zero-length memmove in cspace

g/G with a never-written hold space pass hs (still NULL) with
hsl == 0 to cspace(), which called memmove(dst, NULL, 0) -- undefined
behaviour per the nonnull attribute, caught by UBSan when the
asan+ubsan CI job replayed the grown sedcompile corpus through the
real binary (11 inputs, status 134).  x already special-cases the
NULL hold space (process.c:255); g/G relied on the count being zero.

Guard on len > 0, the same idiom as the ed/sort zero-length memcpy
fixes.  Behaviour unchanged (a zero-length append is a no-op); the
hold space semantics for g/G/x are covered by new regression tests in
12-regress-tools.sh, and the full corpus leg replays clean under
ASan/UBSan.
---
 src.freebsd/sed/process.c | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src.freebsd/sed/process.c b/src.freebsd/sed/process.c
index 08281a8f9..3257ba6ff 100644
--- a/src.freebsd/sed/process.c
+++ b/src.freebsd/sed/process.c
@@ -765,7 +765,11 @@ cspace(SPACE *sp, const char *p, size_t len, enum e_spflag spflag)
 	if (spflag == REPLACE)
 		sp->len = 0;
 
-	memmove(sp->space + sp->len, p, len);
+	/* len == 0 callers may legitimately pass a NULL p (e.g. g/G with an
+	 * untouched hold space); memmove with a NULL argument is UB even
+	 * when the count is zero. */
+	if (len > 0)
+		memmove(sp->space + sp->len, p, len);
 
 	sp->space[sp->len += len] = '\0';
 }
